## Architecture

The engine separates **data, logic, and rendering**:

```text
Game Loop
   │
   ▼
World / ECS
   │
   ├── PlayerSystem
   ├── PhysicsSystem
   ├── CollisionSystem
   └── AnimationSystem
   │
   ▼
Components
   │
   ▼
Camera → Renderer → Canvas
```

An entity is just a collection of components:

```text
Player
├── Transform
├── Velocity
├── Collider
├── Sprite
└── Animation
```

A platform might only have:

```text
Platform
├── Transform
└── Collider
```

This makes the engine easy to extend with components such as `Health`, `Inventory`, `AI`, `ParticleEmitter`, or `AudioSource`.

## Extending to 3D

The ECS can remain largely unchanged. Replace 2D spatial components with 3D versions:

```javascript
Transform
├── position: Vector3
├── rotation: Quaternion
└── scale: Vector3
```

Then replace Canvas 2D rendering with **WebGL2/WebGPU**:

```text
Entity
  ↓
Transform
  ↓
Model Matrix
  ↓
View Matrix
  ↓
Projection Matrix
  ↓
GPU
```

Physics evolves from AABBs to 3D volumes such as boxes, spheres, capsules, and eventually raycasts/continuous collision detection.

Sprite animation can become skeletal animation:

```text
Skeleton
├── Root
├── Spine
├── Head
├── Arm
└── Leg
```

The key is keeping **ECS and gameplay systems independent from the renderer**. That lets the same architecture grow from a small Canvas 2D engine into a WebGL/WebGPU 3D engine.

