## The two bugs, explained

**Bug 1 — skipped last item (`slice(start, end)` off-by-one)**

`Array.prototype.slice(begin, end)` already treats `end` as *exclusive* — it stops **before** that index. So to get `pageSize` items starting at `start`, the correct exclusive end is simply `start + pageSize`.

The original code computed `end = start + pageSize - 1`, treating `end` as *inclusive* (like you'd do with `substring` in some other languages, or 1-based math). Since `slice` is exclusive, that extra `-1` chops off the last real item of every page. It only *looks* fine on the last page because there often aren't enough items left to reach the boundary, so the missing item isn't visually obvious — but it's still cutting the range short.

**Fix:** drop the `-1`.
```js
const end = start + pageSize; // exclusive, correct
```

**Bug 2 — duplicated first item on back navigation**

This comes from inconsistent indexing between "next" and "previous" logic elsewhere in the app: one path treats `pageNum` as 0-indexed (`start = pageNum * pageSize`), while a "go back" handler typically does something like `pageNum = pageNum - 1` but was written assuming 1-indexed pages (or vice versa) — e.g. mixing `currentPage - 1` in one place and `currentPage` in another when recomputing `start`. That mismatch shifts `start` back by less than a full `pageSize`, so the last page's first item reappears at the top of the "previous" page.

**Fix:** pick one convention and use it everywhere. Below, `currentPage` is always 0-indexed internally, and *every* navigation action (`prev`, `next`, clicking a page button) goes through the *same* `goToPage(n)` function — no separate arithmetic paths that can drift out of sync.

```js
function getPage(items, pageSize, pageNum) {
  const start = pageNum * pageSize;
  const end = start + pageSize; // exclusive — no -1 needed
  return items.slice(start, end);
}
```

## Complete implementation

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Pagination Demo</title>
<style>
  body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; max-width: 640px; margin: 40px auto; padding: 0 16px; color: #222; }
  h1 { font-size: 1.3rem; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 16px 0; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 8px 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; }
  th { background: #f5f5f5; }
  #summary { font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444; margin-bottom: 10px; }
  #controls { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; align-items: center; margin-top: 12px; }
  button {
    border: 1px solid #ccc; background: #fff; padding: 6px 12px;
    border-radius: 6px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  button:hover:not(:disabled) { background: #f0f0f0; }
  button:disabled { opacity: 0.4; cursor: default; }
  button.active { background: #2563eb; color: #fff; border-color: #2563eb; }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Pagination Demo (47 items)</h1>
<div id="summary"></div>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Item</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody id="tbody"></tbody>
</table>
<div id="controls"></div>

<script>
  // Hardcoded 47-item dataset
  const items = Array.from({ length: 47 }, (_, i) => `Item ${i + 1}`);
  const pageSize = 10;
  const pageCount = Math.ceil(items.length / pageSize);

  // currentPage is always 0-indexed. Every navigation action funnels
  // through goToPage(), so there's no second code path that can
  // compute a different (wrong) start index.
  let currentPage = 0;

  function getPage(items, pageSize, pageNum) {
    const start = pageNum * pageSize;
    const end = start + pageSize; // exclusive end — fixed off-by-one
    return items.slice(start, end);
  }

  function goToPage(n) {
    currentPage = Math.min(Math.max(n, 0), pageCount - 1); // clamp
    render();
  }

  function render() {
    const pageItems = getPage(items, pageSize, currentPage);

    // --- table body ---
    const tbody = document.getElementById('tbody');
    tbody.innerHTML = pageItems
      .map((item, i) => {
        const globalIndex = currentPage * pageSize + i + 1;
        return `<tr><td>${globalIndex}</td><td>${item}</td></tr>`;
      })
      .join('');

    // --- summary line, accurate at every boundary ---
    const shownCount = pageItems.length;
    const rangeStart = currentPage * pageSize + 1;
    const rangeEnd = currentPage * pageSize + shownCount;
    const remaining = items.length - rangeEnd;

    document.getElementById('summary').textContent =
      `${shownCount} shown / ${remaining} remaining / showing ${rangeStart}\u2013${rangeEnd} of ${items.length}`;

    // --- controls: Prev, numbered buttons, Next ---
    const controls = document.getElementById('controls');
    controls.innerHTML = '';

    const prevBtn = document.createElement('button');
    prevBtn.textContent = 'Prev';
    prevBtn.disabled = currentPage === 0;
    prevBtn.onclick = () => goToPage(currentPage - 1);
    controls.appendChild(prevBtn);

    for (let p = 0; p < pageCount; p++) {
      const btn = document.createElement('button');
      btn.textContent = p + 1; // display 1-indexed, store 0-indexed
      if (p === currentPage) btn.classList.add('active');
      btn.onclick = () => goToPage(p);
      controls.appendChild(btn);
    }

    const nextBtn = document.createElement('button');
    nextBtn.textContent = 'Next';
    nextBtn.disabled = currentPage === pageCount - 1;
    nextBtn.onclick = () => goToPage(currentPage + 1);
    controls.appendChild(nextBtn);
  }

  render();
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Why the boundaries work out

- **First page:** `start = 0`, `end = 10` → items 1–10 shown, no duplication, `Prev` disabled.
- **Middle pages:** each page gets a full, non-overlapping `pageSize` slice because `start`/`end` are derived solely from `currentPage`, the single source of truth.
- **Final page (47 items, pageSize 10 → page 5 = index 4):** `start = 40`, `end = 50`, but `slice` clamps to the array length, so you correctly get items 41–47 (7 items) — the summary computes `remaining = 47 - 47 = 0` and `shown = 7`, no off-by-one loss.
- **Going backward from the last page:** because `goToPage` is the only place `currentPage` changes, and `getPage` always recomputes `start` fresh from that single value, there's no stale/second calculation that could re-include an item from the page you just left — eliminating the duplication bug.

