Here's the cascade problem in short: `.card` has specificity **(0,1,0)** and `.card.active` has **(0,2,0)**, so normally `.active` wins. But `!important` doesn't care about specificity at all — it just wins outright over any non-`!important` rule, regardless of how many classes the other selector has. So on mobile, the flat `4px !important` on `.card` clobbers the more specific `.card.active` rule, and "active" cards visually collapse to the same padding as inactive ones.

The fix: drop `!important` and let specificity do its job — `.card.active` (0,2,0) will always beat `.card` (0,1,0) on its own. Then add an explicit mobile override *for* `.card.active` too, so active cards still get an intentionally different (but still distinct-looking) mobile style, instead of silently inheriting the desktop 16px.

